WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.500
Compatibility is a security and reliability topic for reusable C++ libraries.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:13.960
Consumers may use the library as headers, static libraries, shared libraries, plugins, or package-manager artifacts.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:22.540
Each model has different expectations around public headers, exported symbols, binary compatibility, build flags, and platform-specific behavior.

4
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.680
A change that is harmless for source consumers may be disruptive for binary consumers.

5
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.680
A public header is a long-lived contract.

6
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:40.620
Types, inline code, macros, templates, exceptions, default parameters, and conditional compilation can all affect consumers.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.760
ABI stability requires deliberate policy because small implementation changes can break shared-library consumers or plugins.

8
00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:54.100
If the library offers a binary boundary, the team should define what is stable and what is internal.

9
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.520
Exported symbols and visibility settings should be reviewed as part of that policy.

10
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.440
Versioning should help consumers update safely.

11
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:09.540
Semantic versioning, release notes, deprecation policy, feature flags, and migration guides all reduce surprise.

12
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:17.480
Opaque handles or C-compatible boundaries can reduce binary coupling in some cases, but they still need clear ownership and error contracts.

13
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.380
Optional features should be described so consumers know what changes when they are enabled.

14
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.320
Deprecation should come with examples and realistic timelines.

15
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.200
Compatibility surprises can delay security updates.

16
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:41.340
If an upgrade breaks builds, changes behavior without warning, or removes an API without a migration path, consumers may pin an older version or disable safety features to keep shipping.

17
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.000
Secure library teams make breaking changes deliberate, documented, tested, and paired with a realistic adoption path.

18
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:52.180
They also maintain patch branches when consumers need time to move.

19
00:01:52.280 --> 00:02:02.680
Compatibility tests should cover the supported consumer patterns, not only the library's own examples and internal builds across platforms and package formats used by consumers.
